course: dictionary
lesson: 15006
title: Resentment: the feeling of unfair treatment
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Resentment is a feeling of anger and hurt that grows when you think you have been treated unfairly. It is a noun, and it is usually uncountable, so you say some resentment, not a resentment.
explain: «When the teacher picked Sam for the team again, Ruby felt resentment towards him.» The clues are picked again and felt. Ruby was left out again and again, so she felt hurt and angry about it. Try your meaning in the sentence: "Ruby felt anger and hurt because she thought she was treated unfairly." It still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: Resentment can last a long time. «He held on to his bitter resentment for years after the argument.» The word years tells you the feeling did not go away quickly. Bitter makes it stronger still.
fact: Resentment means anger and hurt because you think you were treated unfairly.
fact: You can say «feel resentment» or «resentment towards» a person, such as resentment towards a sibling or a teammate.
fact: «Bitter resentment» means strong resentment that has lasted a long time.
fact: Forgiveness can help a person let resentment go.
exercise: «When Ben won the prize that Kim had worked for, Kim felt resentment.» What is resentment? | A kind of loud laugh | Great joy at a surprise party | *Anger and hurt at being treated unfairly | A sleepy feeling after a long run
exercise: «Tom still felt bitter resentment after ten years.» Which word shows the feeling has lasted a long time? | Tom | *Still | Felt | Bitter
exercise: Ava says, «Let's forgive Jo and let go of the resentment.» What does forgiving help you do? | Win the next race | Forget your own name | *Let the anger and hurt go | Make the anger grow bigger
test: Which phrase is correct? | *some resentment | three resentments | a resentment | many resentment
test: «Priya's resentment grew each time her ideas were ignored.» What caused the resentment? | Her ideas winning the class vote | The sun shining on the playground | *Her ideas being ignored, which felt unfair | Her new shoes
exam: Forgiveness can help a person do what with resentment? | Write it in capital letters | Make it bigger and stronger | Hold on to it for years | *Let it go
